  "runtimeKernelsNote": "kernel_selection_contract_v1 registry, extended in Phase P1C from the single P1B baseline candidate to a frozen 8-candidate tile/schedule family for the same fused_matmul_bias_relu op (identical semantics, dtype f32, backend cpu, one thread, same compiler flags -- only block_m/block_n/block_k differ; see DOC/result/P1C_*.md for the full working-set-vs-Cortex-A76-cache rationale). IMPORTANT, VERIFIED ARCHITECTURAL LIMITATION (audited in Phase P1C, not fabricated, re-confirmed unchanged in P1C.1): KernelSelectionPass matches candidates on (op_name, backend, dtype, quant_mode, layout, tile-plan, local-memory) and picks the FIRST full match in declaration order, short-circuiting the search -- it has NO signal that varies with an op's concrete (M,N,K) shape, so it cannot and does not perform shape-aware or cache-aware candidate selection today. Declaring a supportedTileShapes constraint per candidate would not fix this: TilePlanningPass never produces a tile plan on this profile (staticCostProfile.localMemoryBytes is deliberately undeclared, per the P1A decision that this Cortex-A76's hardware-managed cache is not a software-managed scratchpad), so every candidate's tile-shape check would defer (deferred_missing_tile_plan) rather than select. Consequently, declaration ORDER is the only lever this compiler has for choosing a default on this profile. PHASE P1C.1 CHANGE: bm32_bn128_bk32 is now declared FIRST (previously bm32_bn32_bk32). This is an evidence-backed LOW-REGRET STATIC DEFAULT change, not a dynamic or shape-aware selector: P1C's Raspberry Pi measurement (432/432 correct, 3 independent performance-governor-pinned sessions, 19 held-out+calibration timed workloads) found bm32_bn128_bk32 has the lowest mean-of-medians latency and the most raw wins both across the full dataset AND when the ranking is restricted to calibration-only workloads (13/24 wins, re-verified in the P1C.1 leakage review -- the choice does not depend on held-out labels), and achieves 0.078% mean regret against the measured oracle on held-out workloads (vs 3.889% mean regret for the previous bm32_bn32_bk32 default) -- see DOC/result/P1C1_*.md. Zero of 17 measured workloads met the pre-registered stable-winner bar (same winner in all 3 sessions with >=5% margin): this remains an aggregate low-regret static preference, not a demonstrated per-shape decision boundary, and not a claim of universal optimality for every possible shape. For ANY workload shape, this profile's live compiler selection is, and will remain, bm32_bn128_bk32 -- the other 7 candidates (including the former default, bm32_bn32_bk32) stay declared so Runtime can still recognize and dispatch them by exact ID, and so future oracle/regret work can still invoke them directly, the same methodology Phase 1/R1's CPU schedule-discovery tool used, independent of live compiler selection. The schema/pass also cannot retain 'candidates considered but not selected' when a match succeeds (the match loop breaks on the first full match), so only the winning descriptor's identity is ever exported in kernel_selection.* -- documented here rather than fabricated. ml-platform-capabilities is not consulted by this declaration (existing architectural bypass predating P1B, unchanged here)."
